course: dictionary
lesson: 8824
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words change meaning depending on where you are, and the words around them give you the clue. «Sherbet has a dry acid and baking soda that only react when wet, so it fizzes in your mouth and not in the packet.» Here sherbet is a fizzy sweet, because the sentence tells you it fizzes when it gets wet, like the wet inside of your mouth.
explain: In the UK, Australia, New Zealand and Ireland, sherbet is a sweet powder you eat, often dipped with a lollipop or liquorice. In the USA, sherbet is a frozen fruit dessert, a bit like soft ice cream. If someone says «sherbet» and you are not sure which one they mean, look at the other words in the sentence.
explain: Science helps explain the fizz. The acid and the baking soda react when they meet water, and the reaction makes bubbles. That is why the sherbet waits quietly in the packet but fizzes in your mouth.
fact: Sherbet is a noun, and it is uncountable, so we say some sherbet, not two sherbets.
fact: In the UK, «sherbet» means a sweet fizzy powder, often eaten with a lollipop or liquorice to dip.
fact: In the USA, «sherbet» means a frozen fruit dessert, like a soft ice cream, made with a little milk.
fact: Sherbet fizzes because its acid and baking soda react with water.
fact: Phrases to know: «sherbet dip», «sherbet lemon», «orange sherbet».
exercise: «Sherbet has a dry acid and baking soda that only react when wet.» What does the sentence say sherbet contains? | Sugar and sea water | Milk and frozen fruit | Flour and eggs | *A dry acid and baking soda
exercise: Kofi asks for a sherbet dip at the shop. What is a sherbet dip? | A type of bread roll | *A sweet fizzy powder you dip a lollipop or liquorice into | A frozen fruit dessert like soft ice cream | A fizzy drink in a bottle
exercise: An American says, «I'd like orange sherbet for dessert.» What is sherbet here? | A chocolate sweet | A kind of fizzy lemonade | *A frozen fruit dessert, like soft ice cream, made with a little milk | A fizzy powder you eat with a lollipop
test: Why does sherbet fizz in your mouth but not in the packet? | Because it is too sweet | Because it is heated by the sun | Because the packet is too tight | *Because it reacts with the water in your mouth
test: Which of these is a sweet in the UK? | *Sherbet lemon | Sherbet bread | Sherbet soup | Frozen sherbet
exam: Which meaning of sherbet fits a child buying sweets in a UK shop? | A pot of hot soup | A type of bread | *A sweet fizzy powder eaten as a sweet | A frozen dessert with milk
